-solution "Let's recall the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978414/assets/d7984b9098d02bfc2b1e9a422aef919533abce54fbb94117e709c7613b0870a3.png)\n\nNote that:\n\n- The function is defined for all real numbers.\n\nTherefore, all the given values are in the domain of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111293 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f045b43-4785-5989-8b5a-dcb8401d1bb3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Points that Lie on the Graph of the Inverse Tangent Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b113f0a9-895a-56a3-8759-b6c2959ff8aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109854} {:answer/id #uuid "bf303d45-b0dd-5d6d-a581-736ceb085d1c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109855} {:answer/id #uuid "c3a086e3-1d0d-522d-a5d7-a925c56f89cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109856} {:answer/id #uuid "08f55e75-52ef-560e-b53a-8c9c154bd4b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109857} {:answer/id #uuid "4bdf6e3b-0b34-539c-933c-fbf55734dae3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109858}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c3a086e3-1d0d-522d-a5d7-a925c56f89cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109856} :answer-field/id #uuid "c79a2455-4317-550b-bb8d-c5b6422cc769" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109853}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b81fb291-176e-5b1d-97d6-f57c92461362" :question/math-academy-id "q-65990" :question/problem "Which of the following points lies on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x?$\n\n- $(1,\\frac{\\pi }{6})$\n- $(\\sqrt{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3})$\n- $(-1,-\\frac{\\pi }{4})$" :question/worked-solution "In order for a point $(a,b)$ to lie on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x,$ the following conditions must hold:\n\n- The reflected point $(b,a)$ must lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$\n\n- The $y$-value of the point must lie in the range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x,$ which is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of the points. \n\n- First,  we consider the point $(1,\\frac{\\pi }{6}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{\\pi }{6},1)$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$  $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(1,\\frac{\\pi }{6})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$\n\n- Next, we consider the point $(\\sqrt{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\sqrt{3})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$ is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ and $y=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\sqrt{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$\n\n- Finally, we consider the point $(-1,-\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(-\\frac{\\pi }{4},-1)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$ is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ and $y=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(-1,-\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ does lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111295 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1587e39e-c7bf-5385-b726-a1b497f7c772" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Values in the Range of the Inverse Tangent Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9ab8dd45-1a1f-5a0d-b216-f0c2d7bd56b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3π}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109866} {:answer/id #uuid "2a7bd78b-d62c-53eb-98ba-8a1ee672557f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109867} {:answer/id #uuid "56115af8-7a43-533b-acbd-fe19aaaea5c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109868} {:answer/id #uuid "624e5038-bd23-53c7-ba41-1dd9aabee23f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109869} {:answer/id #uuid "64e100ab-375e-541b-937d-8c34370a0b10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109870}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "56115af8-7a43-533b-acbd-fe19aaaea5c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109868} :answer-field/id #uuid "74e4b795-121b-51c1-b7c5-b08f34ecab19" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109865}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f9dab244-7d73-533a-8f4f-702eac1aa366" :question/math-academy-id "q-66008" :question/problem "Which of the following values is in the range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978414/assets/d7984b9098d02bfc2b1e9a422aef919533abce54fbb94117e709c7613b0870a3.png)\n\nNote that:\n\n- The range of the function is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\nTherefore, of the given values, only $y=\\frac{1}{4}$ is in the range of the function."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278500 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f045b43-4785-5989-8b5a-dcb8401d1bb3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Points that Lie on the Graph of the Inverse Tangent Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "37b87e1c-1246-5a69-bfa1-1fabe99c00fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278502} {:answer/id #uuid "1bf70dbf-b85e-5c28-b7f4-a3efc0ca6799" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278503} {:answer/id #uuid "c31f4cff-8e11-5baa-abb3-fcd85423ab1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278504} {:answer/id #uuid "ec6fb2f4-6409-5f8c-9de1-0877bd94f296" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278505} {:answer/id #uuid "b6531706-52db-5400-949f-ecbd6d0fe366" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278506}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1bf70dbf-b85e-5c28-b7f4-a3efc0ca6799" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278503} :answer-field/id #uuid "fada6eb1-77b5-567e-be9e-3c7e7ee41126" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278501}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4f72c98d-40b3-587a-b743-ac6b3ce0ce19" :question/math-academy-id "q-70788" :question/problem "Which of the following points lies on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x?$\n\n- $(\\sqrt{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3})$\n- $(0,\\frac{\\pi }{6})$\n- $(-\\sqrt{3},\\frac{2\\pi }{3})$" :question/worked-solution "In order for a point $(a,b)$ to lie on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x,$ the following conditions must hold:\n\n- The reflected point $(b,a)$ must lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$\n\n- The $y$-value of the point must lie in the range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x,$ which is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of the points.  \n\n- First,  we consider the point $(\\sqrt{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\sqrt{3})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$ is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ and $y=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\sqrt{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$\n\n- Next, we consider the point $(0,\\frac{\\pi }{6}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{\\pi }{6},0)$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$  $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(0,\\frac{\\pi }{6})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$\n\n- Finally, we consider the point $(-\\sqrt{3},\\frac{2\\pi }{3}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{2\\pi }{3},-\\sqrt{3})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$ is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ and $y=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ does **not** lie within this range. $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(-\\sqrt{3},\\frac{2\\pi }{3})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$"}]}
